      "ts": "2026-09-17T10:40:38.668749-07:00",
      "seq": 76,
      "game_seq": 231,
      "reasoning": null,
      "thinking": "Waterbender is still in combat despite being tapped, since tapping after attack declaration doesn't remove it from combat, so both creatures are still attacking. With only 2 untapped lands, I'm weighing whether to use Reversal on Waterbender or on Snowballers, but since Ty Lee already keeps Waterbender locked down as long as she survives, destroying it might be redundant.\n\
\n\
If I destroy Waterbender, Ty Lee (3/2 with prowess) can then block Snowballers, trading with it since 2 damage kills her 2 toughness. Alternatively, destroying Snowballers and letting Ty Lee take 2 unblocked damage from Waterbender keeps her alive and preserves the lock, though the opponent still has 5 open lands and one card in hand. I only have 2 mana, so I can't cast Yip Yip and Reversal together — I have to pick one line: destroy Waterbender and trade Ty Lee into Snowballers, leaving the opponent empty-handed on board, or destroy Snowballers instead.\n\
\n\
Actually, casting Yip Yip on Ty Lee for just W turns her into a 5/4 (with prowess), letting her kill Snowballers and survive while I take 2 from Waterbender, and this is cheaper than Reversal so I still keep that card for later — the main risk is the opponent using their remaining card and 5 mana to remove Ty Lee in response, which would let Waterbender untap next turn. Still, this option seems like the best value since it kills Snowballers, keeps Ty Lee alive, and holds onto Reversal.\n\
\n\
I could also sequence this more safely: block with Ty Lee first, then cast Yip Yip during the declare blockers step after seeing if the opponent reacts, giving them fewer chances to respond with tricks. That seems like the safer play, so I'll pass now and block first.\n\
\n\
",
